<translate>
<!--T:1-->
{{SITENAME}} runs in two ways. On Linux the standalone binary is the simplest, a single file with no Docker. On macOS or Windows, use the Docker image. To run a change of your own, or a commit that no release carries yet, [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Building from source</tvar>|build it from source]] instead.

<!--T:2-->
<span id="binary"></span>
== Binary ==

<!--T:3-->
A single self-contained executable that embeds MediaWiki, for Linux on x86_64 and arm64. Download it and make it executable:
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="shell" copy>
curl -L -o wikven https://github.com/chaotic-ground/wikven/releases/latest/download/wikven-linux-x86_64
chmod +x wikven
</syntaxhighlight>

<translate>
<!--T:4-->
On arm64, use <code>wikven-linux-aarch64</code>. See [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Standalone binary</tvar>|Standalone binary]] for installing it onto your <code>PATH</code>, nightly builds, and the optional host tools it uses.
</translate>

<span id="Docker"></span>
== Docker ==

<translate>
<!--T:6-->
Works on any platform with [https://www.docker.com/ Docker], so it is the option to use on macOS and Windows. The image is published to the GitHub Container Registry:
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="shell" copy>
docker pull ghcr.io/chaotic-ground/wikven
</syntaxhighlight>

<translate>
<!--T:12-->
The same image is published to Quay as well, at <code>quay.io/chaotic-ground/wikven</code>. One build pushes both addresses, so pull from whichever one answers.

<!--T:7-->
The untagged image is the newest released version. To hold a build still, pin the version tag <code>:<tvar name="1">{{WIKVENVERSION}}</tvar></code> or the digest (<code>ghcr.io/chaotic-ground/wikven@sha256:...</code>); shorter tags for the major and the minor version are published too, and move as that line gains releases. A dated <code>nightly-YYYY-MM-DD</code> tag is the main branch as it stood that day, for trying a change before it is released.

<!--T:13-->
== Which MediaWiki ==

<!--T:14-->
You do not install MediaWiki: every way of running {{SITENAME}} brings its own. The image and the binary are built on '''MediaWiki {{CURRENTVERSION}}''', and that is what renders your pages however you run them.

<!--T:15-->
<span id="versions"></span>
== Versions ==

<!--T:16-->
A new major version of {{SITENAME}} comes with every MediaWiki release it moves to, and only then. It follows each MediaWiki release rather than only the long-term-support ones, which comes to about two majors a year. A new MediaWiki can change the HTML your <code>MediaWiki:Common.css</code> and <code>MediaWiki:Common.js</code> select against, so read a major's [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Release notes</tvar>|release notes]] before moving to it, and build your site and look at it afterwards.

<!--T:17-->
A change of {{SITENAME}}'s own that would break a site does not get a major of its own. It waits for the next MediaWiki release, and until then the old behaviour keeps working and the documentation says what is coming, so a minor or patch release does not break a site that built before it. The one exception is a change that fixes something harmful, which does not wait.
</translate>

